## Downloading the data

The data were collected from accelerometers placed on participants' belts,
arms, forearms and dumbbells. This document downloads the official training and
testing files automatically.

```{r download-data}
training_url <- paste0(
  "https://d396qusza40orc.cloudfront.net/",
  "predmachlearn/pml-training.csv"
)

testing_url <- paste0(
  "https://d396qusza40orc.cloudfront.net/",
  "predmachlearn/pml-testing.csv"
)

training_file <- "pml-training.csv"
testing_file  <- "pml-testing.csv"

if (!file.exists(training_file)) {
  download.file(training_url, training_file, mode = "wb")
}

if (!file.exists(testing_file)) {
  download.file(testing_url, testing_file, mode = "wb")
}

training_raw <- read.csv(
  training_file,
  na.strings = c("NA", "", "#DIV/0!"),
  check.names = TRUE
)

testing_raw <- read.csv(
  testing_file,
  na.strings = c("NA", "", "#DIV/0!"),
  check.names = TRUE
)

data.frame(
  data_set = c("Training", "Course test"),
  rows = c(nrow(training_raw), nrow(testing_raw)),
  columns = c(ncol(training_raw), ncol(testing_raw))
)
```

## Data cleaning

The first seven fields contain row numbers, participant information,
timestamps and window identifiers. These variables describe the experimental
schedule rather than the physical movement itself, so they were excluded.

Many calculated summary variables are almost completely missing. I retained
only sensor variables observed in at least 95% of the training rows.

```{r cleaning}
administrative_columns <- c(
  "X",
  "user_name",
  "raw_timestamp_part_1",
  "raw_timestamp_part_2",
  "cvtd_timestamp",
  "new_window",
  "num_window",
  "problem_id"
)

candidate_predictors <- setdiff(
  names(training_raw),
  c(administrative_columns, "classe")
)

observed_fraction <- vapply(
  training_raw[candidate_predictors],
  function(x) mean(!is.na(x)),
  numeric(1)
)

predictors <- names(
  observed_fraction[observed_fraction >= 0.95]
)

model_data <- training_raw[c(predictors, "classe")]
model_data$classe <- factor(model_data$classe)

course_test <- testing_raw[predictors]

data.frame(
  training_observations = nrow(model_data),
  retained_predictors = length(predictors),
  outcome_classes = nlevels(model_data$classe)
)
```

## Outcome distribution

```{r class-distribution, echo=FALSE}
class_counts <- table(model_data$classe)

barplot(
  class_counts,
  col = "#2C7FB8",
  border = NA,
  main = "Training Observations by Outcome Class",
  xlab = "Classe",
  ylab = "Number of Observations"
)
```

The classes are not perfectly balanced, but every class contains several
thousand observations. Stratified sampling was used so that the development and
validation sets maintained approximately the same class proportions.

## Training and validation split

I reserved 25% of the labelled observations for final validation. These
observations were not used for model fitting or hyperparameter selection.

```{r data-split}
set.seed(123)

development_rows <- createDataPartition(
  model_data$classe,
  p = 0.75,
  list = FALSE
)

development_data <- model_data[development_rows, ]
validation_data  <- model_data[-development_rows, ]

data.frame(
  sample = c("Development", "Validation"),
  observations = c(
    nrow(development_data),
    nrow(validation_data)
  )
)
```

## Model selection and cross-validation

A random forest was selected because the relationships among the sensor
measurements are nonlinear and likely contain interactions. Random forests also
work without scaling the predictors.

Within the development sample, stratified five-fold cross-validation was used
to compare three values of `mtry`. Accuracy was used as the selection metric.
Parallel processing was disabled to improve compatibility with the Coursera
lab environment.

```{r train-random-forest}
set.seed(123)

cv_control <- trainControl(
  method = "cv",
  number = 5,
  allowParallel = FALSE
)

tuning_grid <- expand.grid(
  mtry = c(2, 8, 16)
)

rf_model <- train(
  classe ~ .,
  data = development_data,
  method = "rf",
  metric = "Accuracy",
  trControl = cv_control,
  tuneGrid = tuning_grid,
  ntree = 300
)

knitr::kable(
  rf_model$results[, c("mtry", "Accuracy", "Kappa")],
  digits = 4,
  caption = "Five-fold cross-validation results"
)
```

The selected value of `mtry` was:

```{r selected-model}
rf_model$bestTune
```

## Out-of-sample performance

The selected model was evaluated on the untouched validation sample.

```{r validation-results}
validation_predictions <- predict(
  rf_model,
  newdata = validation_data
)

validation_results <- confusionMatrix(
  validation_predictions,
  validation_data$classe
)

validation_results$table
```

```{r performance-summary}
validation_accuracy <- unname(
  validation_results$overall["Accuracy"]
)

estimated_error <- 1 - validation_accuracy

data.frame(
  validation_accuracy = round(validation_accuracy, 4),
  estimated_out_of_sample_error = round(estimated_error, 4)
)
```

The validation accuracy was
`r sprintf("%.2f%%", 100 * validation_accuracy)`. Therefore, the estimated
out-of-sample error was
`r sprintf("%.2f%%", 100 * estimated_error)`.

This estimate applies to new observations collected under conditions similar
to the original experiment. Performance could differ for other devices or
substantially different populations.

## Final model

After estimating out-of-sample performance, the selected specification was
refitted using the complete labelled dataset. The 20 course test cases were
not used for training or model selection.

```{r final-model}
set.seed(123)

best_mtry <- rf_model$bestTune$mtry

final_model <- randomForest(
  x = model_data[predictors],
  y = model_data$classe,
  mtry = best_mtry,
  ntree = 500,
  importance = TRUE
)
```

## Important predictors

```{r variable-importance, echo=FALSE}
importance_values <- importance(final_model)

importance_order <- order(
  importance_values[, "MeanDecreaseGini"],
  decreasing = TRUE
)

top_variables <- head(importance_order, 15)

barplot(
  rev(importance_values[top_variables, "MeanDecreaseGini"]),
  names.arg = rev(rownames(importance_values)[top_variables]),
  horiz = TRUE,
  las = 1,
  col = "#41AB5D",
  border = NA,
  main = "Fifteen Most Important Predictors",
  xlab = "Mean Decrease in Gini"
)
```

## Predictions for the 20 quiz cases

```{r quiz-predictions}
quiz_predictions <- predict(
  final_model,
  newdata = course_test
)

prediction_table <- data.frame(
  problem_id = testing_raw$problem_id,
  prediction = as.character(quiz_predictions)
)

knitr::kable(
  prediction_table,
  caption = "Predictions for the course quiz"
)

write.csv(
  prediction_table,
  "predictions.csv",
  row.names = FALSE
)
```

For convenient quiz entry, the predictions in order are:

```{r prediction-sequence}
paste(quiz_predictions, collapse = " ")
```

## Conclusion

The random forest achieved high predictive accuracy on data that were kept
separate from model training. The combination of five-fold cross-validation
and independent validation reduces the risk of selecting a model based only on
its training performance. The final model was trained using all labelled
observations before generating the 20 course predictions.
